Woman bare the mortal burden of having to give birth, we absolutely have to appreciate and respect that, I agree. 

&amp;#x200B;

But it is worth pointing out that as culture get's more technologically advanced, the rate of death associated with child birth is decreasing worldwide (e.g. 44% in the last 25 years)

[https://data.unicef.org/topic/maternal-health/maternal-mortality/](https://data.unicef.org/topic/maternal-health/maternal-mortality/)



On the flip side the increase in population and technology together have created situations where resources become more and more scarce. The pressure to conquer neighboring territory for domestic gain has been a growing concern. Just think about WW1 and WW2.

&amp;#x200B;

Just like we ought to appreciate and respect woman for child birth we need to do the same for the men who've died trying to protect their wife's, daughters, mothers and sisters. I'm not justifying war, I'm just appreciating that it seemed necessary at some point in time and that 99% of the people who were sent to the meat grinder in the name of their countries were male. 

&amp;#x200B;

Hopefully some day war becomes a relic of history and hopefully some day deaths during childbirth get's reduced to statistical insignificance.

I disagree with you on what constitutes long term planning. 

&amp;#x200B;

Making fancy cakes requires perhaps short and medium term planning. The motivating drives are both the short term "eating of the delicious high calorie good stuff" as well as the medium term "make it beautiful and decorated" to satisfy my drive for self expression and experience of beauty. 

&amp;#x200B;

The person running around the block (\*and not eating cakes\*) to better their health and longevity is engaging in long term/ higher order planning for 2 reasons. First the idea of "being healthy" is an abstraction only available to human beings. An ape cannot make considerations for a month down the road let alone 20 years down the road. This kind of long term planning necessitates special regions of cortical development in the brain that only humans have. 

&amp;#x200B;

Second the choice not to eat the cakes is another higher order process. I have my abstract goal to "being healthy" and so I will use that theoretical goal to stay diligent and not buy the donuts at the grocery store even though my mouth is watering at the sight of the powdered sugar Etenmann's. 

&amp;#x200B;

My mouth waters at the sight of a sugary treat, that is a base drive I have no higher cognitive control over, e.g. when my eyes come upon a high calorie treat my mouth will water and my stomach will grumble. I cannot use my will power to activate my stomach or my salivary glands in this way (perhaps unless I visualize a sugary treat in my mind). However, I can use my higher cognitive faculties to disregard the signals my body is sending me. I can be mindful of the saliva building up in my mouth and the pain in my stomach and I can choose to instead focus on the higher \*abstract\* goal of "being healthy". 

&amp;#x200B;

If we are going to call these all drives then we should at least be making the distinction that they are very much a different kind of drive. I again would argue that the higher order drive or impulse to follow an abstract long term goal (e.g be healthy) is a function of rationality and is a necessary component of being wise.

