Ok, I appreciate your response it does clarify Zizek for me a bit.  

&amp;#x200B;

Does the book you recommend explain this notion of a materialist reversal of Marx through a return to Hegel? I know a little about both Marx and Hegel, but a "materialist reversal" back to Hegel (who's an idealist) is confusing.

\&gt; when our opponent's only goal is attention

Really? The ONLY goal? 

&amp;#x200B;

\&gt; modern media killed the art of nuanced argumentation a long time ago

What about it's effects on nuanced journalism?

&amp;#x200B;

\&gt; By going through the motions of exchanging ideas with these facsimiles of public debate, we stifle ourselves and the hope of social progress.

&amp;#x200B;

There is some truth to this, but, the author doesn't grant that unlike the talking heads on TV the debate was a civil exchange between people seen as being in opposition to one another. 

&amp;#x200B;

I mean if she really want's high caliber intellectual debates they are available online for free. Zizek and Peterson (although Zizek more than Peterson) are not high level theorists ironing out there ideas in academia. They are smart guys who speak to a broad audience, and, as such will express a lower level of discourse. 

&amp;#x200B;

I don't see anything wrong with the average IQ person getting excited about a 3 hour long conversation. It's certainly better than watching Kardashians. 

&amp;#x200B;

IMO the author comes off very condescending here to the average person. Not everyone is capable of following high level debate on economic theory, it's just not in the cards. Her expectation that the masses should all be Philosopher Kings is naive.
